(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_workenergyandpower=[{id:"neet-2026-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Power",q:"The power of a crane, which lifts a mass of 1000 kg to a height of 20 m in 10 s is: (g = 9.8 m/s\xB2)",o:["19.6 W","39.2 W","39.2 kW","19.6 kW"],c:3,s:`Power = Work/Time.
Work = mgh, so P = mgh/t.
P = (10\xB3 \xD7 9.8 \xD7 20)/10 = 19.6 \xD7 10\xB3 W.
P = 19.6 kW.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q025",pn:25,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Work done by variable friction",q:"A particle of mass M moves along a horizontal x axis from x = 0 to x = L. The coefficient of kinetic friction varies as a function of x as \u03BC\u2096(x) = \u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1x, where \u03BC\u2080, \u03B1 are constants of appropriate dimensions, so that \u03BC\u2096(L) = 0. The total work done by the frictional force during the motion is n\u03BC\u2080MgL, where g is the acceleration due to gravity. The value of n is:",o:["1","1/3","1/2","3"],c:2,s:`Given \u03BC\u2096(x) = \u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1x and \u03BC\u2096(L) = 0 = \u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1L, so \u03BC\u2080 = \u03B1L.
Magnitude of work by friction: W_f = \u222BdW_f.
dW_f = f\u2096dx = \u03BC\u2096N dx = \u03BC\u2096Mg dx = (\u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1x)Mg dx.
W_f = \u222B\u2080\u1D38 \u03BC\u2080Mg dx \u2212 \u222B\u2080\u1D38 \u03B1Mg x dx = \u03BC\u2080Mg[x]\u2080\u1D38 \u2212 \u03B1Mg[x\xB2/2]\u2080\u1D38.
n\u03BC\u2080MgL = \u03BC\u2080MgL \u2212 (\u03BC\u2080/L)Mg(L\xB2/2) = \u03BC\u2080MgL/2.
So n = 1/2.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q033",pn:33,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Elastic collision and energy conservation",q:"Bob B of mass m at rest is hanging vertically from the ceiling via a massless string of length 10 m, as shown in the figure. Point mass A of mass m travelling horizontally with speed 10 ms\u207B\xB9 hits bob B elastically. The bob B rises h meter after the collision. Taking the acceleration due to gravity g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2 and neglecting the size of the bob, the value of h is:",o:["7","5","2.5","8"],c:1,s:`To reach the horizontal position, the bob would need a speed of 10\u221A2 m/s.
In an elastic collision of equal masses, the velocities are interchanged.
So the bob moves with 10 m/s after the collision.
By conservation of energy: mgh = (1)/(2)mv\xB2.
h = v\xB2/(2g).
h = (10)\xB2/(2 \xD7 10).
h = 5 m.`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A bob B hangs from a ceiling on a vertical string; point mass A at the same level on the left moves right at 10 ms\u207B\xB9 toward B.",d:[["l",150,15,190,15,"B"],["l",170,15,170,150],["c",170,150,3,"F"],["t",178,148,"B","s"],["c",110,150,3,"F"],["t",106,144,"A","es"],["l",115,150,145,150,"a"],["t",128,172,"10 ms\u207B\xB9","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Work-energy theorem",q:"The kinetic energies of two similar cars A and B are 100 J and 225 J respectively. On applying breaks, car A stops after 1000 m and car B stops after 1500 m. If F_A and F_B are the forces applied by the breaks on cars A and B respectively, then the ratio of F_A/F_B is",o:["3/2","2/3","1/3","1/2"],c:1,s:`By work-energy theorem, work done by the brakes = change in K.E.
\u2212FS = k_f \u2212 k_i
\u21D2 FS = k_i \u2212 k_f = k_i (car stops, k_f = 0)
\u21D2 F_A/F_B = (k_A/k_B) \xD7 (S_B/S_A)
= (100/225) \xD7 (1500/1000)
= 150/225 = 2/3`},{id:"neet-2025-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Vertical circular motion, slack string",q:"A bob of heavy mass m is suspended by a light string of length l. The bob is given a horizontal velocity v\u2080 as shown in figure. If the string gets slack at some point P making an angle \u03B8 from the horizontal, the ratio of the speed v of the bob at point P to its initial speed v\u2080 is:",o:["(sin\u03B8)^(1/2)","(1/(2 + 3sin\u03B8))^(1/2)","(cos\u03B8/(2 + 3sin\u03B8))^(1/2)","(sin\u03B8/(2 + 3sin\u03B8))^(1/2)"],c:3,s:`At point P the string is slack, so the radial component of weight gives the centripetal force:
mg sin\u03B8 = mv\xB2/l ...(1)
Conservation of mechanical energy between Q (bottom) and P:
(1/2)mv\u2080\xB2 = (1/2)mv\xB2 + mg(l + l sin\u03B8)
v\u2080\xB2/2 = v\xB2/2 + gl(1 + sin\u03B8)
From (1), gl = v\xB2/sin\u03B8. Put this in:
v\u2080\xB2/2 = v\xB2/2 + (v\xB2/sin\u03B8)(1 + sin\u03B8)
v\u2080\xB2/2 = v\xB2/2 + v\xB2/sin\u03B8 + v\xB2
v\u2080\xB2/2 = (3/2)v\xB2 + 2v\xB2/(2 sin\u03B8)
v\u2080\xB2 = v\xB2[3 + 2/sin\u03B8]
v/v\u2080 = (sin\u03B8/(3 sin\u03B8 + 2))^(1/2)`,fig:{w:190,h:190,alt:"A bob m at the bottom of a circle of centre O hangs from a string of length l and is given horizontal velocity v0; point P on the upper right of the circle makes angle theta with the dashed horizontal through O.",d:[["p","M88.2 8.1 A69.3 69.3 0 0 0 9 63",""],["p","M88.2 8.1 A69.3 69.3 0 1 1 9 63","d"],["l",78.3,78.3,78.3,146.7,"B"],["l",78.3,78.3,126,27,"B"],["l",78.3,78.3,150.3,78.3,"d"],["c",126,27,2.3,"F"],["c",78.3,146.7,2.7,"F"],["l",67.5,81.9,67.5,144,"b"],["t",69.3,117,"l",""],["p","M100.8 78.3 A22.5 22.5 0 0 0 93.6 62.1",""],["t",100.8,72.9,"\u03B8",""],["t",69.3,72.9,"O","e"],["t",135,22.5,"P",""],["l",81.9,146.7,117,146.7,"a"],["t",122.4,152.1,"v\u2080",""],["t",78.3,165.6,"m","m"]]},sfig:{w:260,h:215,alt:"Circle with centre O, bottom point Q with velocity v0 to the right; at P up on the right the velocity v is up-left, mg points down, mg sin theta points along PO and the tangent makes 90 - theta with the horizontal.",d:[["c",79.2,96.3,69.3,""],["l",79.2,96.3,79.2,164.7,"B"],["l",79.2,96.3,126.9,46.8,"B"],["l",79.2,96.3,148.5,96.3,"d"],["r",74.7,160.2,9,9,""],["l",83.7,164.7,119.7,164.7,"a"],["t",123.3,170.1,"v\u2080",""],["t",78.3,187.2,"Q","m"],["c",126.9,46.8,1.8,"F"],["l",126.9,46.8,98.1,19.8,"a"],["t",86.4,18.9,"v",""],["l",126.9,46.8,126.9,72,"a"],["t",129,81,"mg","s"],["l",126.9,46.8,107.1,64.8,"a"],["t",50.4,64.8,"mgsin\u03B8",""],["l",126.9,46.8,150.3,46.8,""],["t",155.7,50.4,"90 \u2212 \u03B8",""],["p","M139.5 46.8 A12.6 12.6 0 0 0 135.9 37.8",""],["p","M101.7 96.3 A22.5 22.5 0 0 0 94.5 79.2",""],["t",99.9,90,"\u03B8",""],["t",72,92.7,"O","e"],["t",131,37,"P",""]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Instantaneous power",q:"At any instant of time t, the displacement of any particle is given by 2t \u2013 1 (SI unit) under the influence of force of 5 N. The value of instantaneous power is (in SI unit) :",o:["7","6","10","5"],c:2,s:`x = 2t \u2212 1.
v = dx/dt = 2 m s\u207B\xB9.
P = F\xB7v = 5 \xD7 2 = 10 W.`},{id:"neet-2024-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Inelastic collision",q:"Two bodies A and B of same mass undergo completely inelastic one dimensional collision. The body A moves with velocity v\u2081 while body B is at rest before collision. The velocity of the system after collision is v\u2082. The ratio v\u2081 : v\u2082 is-",o:["4 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 2","2 : 1"],c:3,s:`Before collision A moves with v\u2081 and B is at rest.
The collision is completely inelastic, so they move together with v\u2082.
Momentum is conserved: mv\u2081 = mv\u2082 + mv\u2082 = 2mv\u2082.
So v\u2081/v\u2082 = 2/1.`,sfig:{w:170,h:70,alt:"Before collision: ball A moves with velocity v1 towards ball B which is at rest.",d:[["c",20,25,11],["t",20,29,"A","m"],["l",31,25,66,25,"a"],["t",70,29,"v\u2081"],["c",120,25,11],["t",120,29,"B","m"],["t",120,52,"rest","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Spring potential energy",q:"The potential energy of a long spring when stretched by 2 cm is U. If the spring is stretched by 8 cm, potential energy stored in it will be:",o:["16U","2U","4U","8U"],c:0,s:`Potential energy = \xBDKx\xB2.
At x = 2 cm: U = \xBDK(2)\xB2 = 2K.
At x = 8 cm: U' = \xBDK(8)\xB2 = 32K.
U'/U = 32K/2K = 16, so U' = 16U.`},{id:"neet-2022-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Energy from power",q:"The energy that will be ideally radiated by a 100 kW transmitter in 1 hour is",o:["36 \xD7 10\u2074 J","36 \xD7 10\u2075 J","1 \xD7 10\u2075 J","36 \xD7 10\u2077 J"],c:3,s:`Energy = power \xD7 time.
E = 100 \xD7 10\xB3 W \xD7 3600 s = 36 \xD7 10\u2077 J.`},{id:"neet-2022-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Power of a lift",q:"An electric lift with a maximum load of 2000 kg (lift + passengers) is moving up with a constant speed of 1.5 ms\u207B\xB9. The frictional force opposing the motion is 3000 N. The minimum power delivered by the motor to the lift in watts is : (g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["20000","34500","23500","23000"],c:1,s:`Speed is constant, so the motor force balances weight plus friction.
F = 2000 \xD7 10 + 3000 = 23000 N.
Power = F\xB7v = 23000 \xD7 1.5 = 34500 W.`},{id:"neet-2021-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Conservation of energy",q:"A particle is released from height S from the surface of the Earth. At a certain height its kinetic energy is three times its potential energy. The height from the surface of earth and the speed of the particle at that instant are respectively",o:["S/4, \u221A(3gS/2)","S/4, 3gS/2","S/4, \u221A(3gS)/2","S/2, \u221A(3gS)/2"],c:0,s:`Let the height be y. The particle fell S \u2013 y from rest.
Total energy: mgS = K + U, and K = 3U, so mgS = 4U = 4mgy.
So y = S/4.
K = 3U gives (1)/(2)mv\xB2 = 3mg(S/4).
v\xB2 = 3gS/2, so v = \u221A(3gS/2).`},{id:"neet-2021-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Power",q:"Water falls from a height of 60 m at the rate of 15 kg/s to operate a turbine. The losses due to frictional force are 10% of the input energy. How much power is generated by the turbine? (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["7.0 kW","10.2 kW","8.1 kW","12.3 kW"],c:2,s:`Input power = (dm/dt) g h = 15 \xD7 10 \xD7 60 = 9000 W.
10% is lost, so 90% is delivered.
Output = 0.9 \xD7 9000 = 8100 W = 8.1 kW.`},{id:"neet-2019-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Elastic collisions",q:"Body A of mass 4m moving with speed u collides with another body B of mass 2m, at rest. The collision is head on and elastic in nature. After the collision the fraction of energy lost by the colliding body A is :",o:["4/9","5/9","1/9","8/9"],c:3,s:`For an elastic head-on collision, the velocity of A after it is v1 = (4m \u2212 2m)u/(4m + 2m) = u/3.
B's velocity is v2 = 2(4m)u/(6m) = 4u/3.
Initial KE of A = \xBD(4m)u\xB2 = 2mu\xB2.
Final KE of A = \xBD(4m)(u/3)\xB2 = 2mu\xB2/9.
Fraction lost = 1 \u2212 1/9 = 8/9.`},{id:"neet-2019-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Work by variable force",q:"A force F = 20 + 10y acts on a particle in y-direction where F is in newton and y in meter. Work done by this force to move the particle from y = 0 to y = 1 m is :",o:["25 J","20 J","30 J","5 J"],c:0,s:`Work = \u222BF dy from 0 to 1.
= [20y + 10y\xB2/2] from 0 to 1.
= 20 + 5 = 25 J.`},{id:"neet-2018-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Coefficient of restitution",q:"A moving block having mass m, collides with another stationary block having mass 4m. The lighter block comes to rest after collision. When the initial velocity of the lighter block is v, then the value of coefficient of restitution (e) will be",o:["0.5","0.25","0.8","0.4"],c:1,s:`Momentum conservation: mv = m \xD7 0 + 4m \xD7 v\u2032, so v\u2032 = (v)/(4).
e = (speed of separation)/(speed of approach) = ((v/4) \u2013 0)/(v \u2013 0) = (1)/(4).
e = 0.25.`},{id:"neet-2018-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Vertical circle, minimum height",q:"A body initially at rest and sliding along a frictionless track from a height h (as shown in the figure) just completes a vertical circle of diameter AB = D. height h is equal to",o:["(3)/(2)D","D","(7)/(5)D","(4)/(5)D"],c:3,s:`At the top B of the circle for just completing it: mg = (mv\xB2)/(r), so v\xB2 = rg = (gD)/(2).
Energy conservation from start to B (height D above the ground): mg(h \u2013 D) = (1)/(2)mv\xB2.
So g(h \u2013 D) = (1)/(2) \xD7 (gD)/(2), so h \u2013 D = (D)/(4).
This gives h = (5D)/(4).
Note: Printed answer 4 (4/5 D); my working gives h = 5D/4 (the printed option looks like a typo of 5/4 D). Printed key kept.`,fig:{w:280,h:190,alt:"A track from height h slopes down to point A at the bottom of a vertical circle of diameter AB = D, with B at the top.",d:[["l",40,20,40,160,"b"],["l",40,160,230,160,""],["l",40,20,150,160,""],["l",92,86,98,94,"a"],["c",230,137,23,""],["l",230,114,230,160,"d"],["t",46,95,"h",""],["t",230,106,"B","m"],["t",230,178,"A","m"]]}}]})();
